<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Kitts and Nevis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Federation of Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Christopher and Nevis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and Nevis, officially the Federation of Saint Kitts and Nevis, is an island country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the Lesser Antilles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Saint Kitts hosted the first British and French Caribbean colonies, which divided the island between them after 1625, and its slave-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry made it the richest British colony per capita in the Caribbean by the 1700s. The islands were federated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> in 1882, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation from 1958 to 1962, and became an associated state with full internal autonomy in 1967. Anguilla seceded that year, and the federation of the two remaining islands gained full independence on 19 September 1983.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The federation consists of two main islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and Nevis, separated by a shallow channel called the Narrows, about 3 kilometres wide. Its area is 261 square kilometres, which makes it the smallest sovereign state in the Western Hemisphere in both area and population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basseterre">Basseterre</a>, the capital, lies on Saint Kitts and is its main port for cruise ships and cargo. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a>, who lived on Saint Kitts before the Europeans arrived, called it Liamuiga, roughly 'fertile land'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were pre-ceramic hunter-gatherers who arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> as early as 3000 BCE. Around 100 BCE the Saladoid people, a ceramic-using farming society from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> region, brought agriculture and pottery.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> sighted the islands in 1493. The first permanent European settlers were the English in 1623, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-warner">Thomas Warner</a>, who founded a settlement at Old Road Town after reaching an agreement with the Carib chief Ouboutou Tegremante. The French settled in 1625 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a>, and the two parties partitioned the island. The English began settling Nevis from 1628. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> has been called the Mother Colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, since it hosted the first British and French Caribbean colonies.</p><p>By the end of the 1700s its slave-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry had made it the richest British Crown Colony per capita in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, and it had overtaken Nevis, formerly the richer island. In 1782 the French recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1783 returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Independence came on 19 September 1983, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennedy-simmonds">Kennedy Simmonds</a> as the first Prime Minister. In 1998 a referendum on Nevis voted 62 per cent to secede, short of the two-thirds majority required.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and Nevis is a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and a Commonwealth realm, with Charles III as head of state, represented by a Governor-General. It is divided into fourteen parishes, nine on Saint Kitts and five on Nevis. English is the sole official language, and Saint Kitts Creole is also widely spoken. The 2021 population was 47,606. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brimstone-hill-fortress">Brimstone Hill Fortress</a>, a national park since 1985, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Kitts and Nevis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
